    <enumeration name="Align"
                 glib:type-name="GtkAlign"
                 glib:get-type="gtk_align_get_type"
                 c:type="GtkAlign">
      <doc xml:space="preserve"
           filename="gtk/gtkenums.h"
           line="44">Controls how a widget deals with extra space in a single (x or y)
dimension.

Alignment only matters if the widget receives a “too large” allocation,
for example if you packed the widget with the #GtkWidget:expand
flag inside a #GtkBox, then the widget might get extra space.  If
you have for example a 16x16 icon inside a 32x32 space, the icon
could be scaled and stretched, it could be centered, or it could be
positioned to one side of the space.

Note that in horizontal context @GTK_ALIGN_START and @GTK_ALIGN_END
are interpreted relative to text direction.

GTK_ALIGN_BASELINE support for it is optional for containers and widgets, and
it is only supported for vertical alignment.  When its not supported by
a child or a container it is treated as @GTK_ALIGN_FILL.</doc>
      <member name="fill"
              value="0"
              c:identifier="GTK_ALIGN_FILL"
              glib:nick="fill"
              glib:name="GTK_ALIGN_FILL">
        <doc xml:space="preserve"
             filename="gtk/gtkenums.h"
             line="46">stretch to fill all space if possible, center if
    no meaningful way to stretch</doc>
      </member>
      <member name="start"
              value="1"
              c:identifier="GTK_ALIGN_START"
              glib:nick="start"
              glib:name="GTK_ALIGN_START">
        <doc xml:space="preserve"
             filename="gtk/gtkenums.h"
             line="48">snap to left or top side, leaving space on right
    or bottom</doc>
      </member>
      <member name="end"
              value="2"
              c:identifier="GTK_ALIGN_END"
              glib:nick="end"
              glib:name="GTK_ALIGN_END">
        <doc xml:space="preserve"
             filename="gtk/gtkenums.h"
             line="50">snap to right or bottom side, leaving space on left
    or top</doc>
      </member>
      <member name="center"
              value="3"
              c:identifier="GTK_ALIGN_CENTER"
              glib:nick="center"
              glib:name="GTK_ALIGN_CENTER">
        <doc xml:space="preserve"
             filename="gtk/gtkenums.h"
             line="52">center natural width of widget inside the
    allocation</doc>
      </member>
      <member name="baseline"
              value="4"
              c:identifier="GTK_ALIGN_BASELINE"
              glib:nick="baseline"
              glib:name="GTK_ALIGN_BASELINE">
        <doc xml:space="preserve"
             filename="gtk/gtkenums.h"
             line="54">align the widget according to the baseline. Since 3.10.</doc>
      </member>
    </enumeration>
    <class name="Alignment"
           c:symbol-prefix="alignment"
           c:type="GtkAlignment"
           parent="Bin"
           glib:type-name="GtkAlignment"
           glib:get-type="gtk_alignment_get_type"
           glib:type-struct="AlignmentClass">
      <doc xml:space="preserve"
           filename="gtk/deprecated/gtkalignment.c"
           line="25">The #GtkAlignment widget controls the alignment and size of its child widget.
It has four settings: xscale, yscale, xalign, and yalign.

The scale settings are used to specify how much the child widget should
expand to fill the space allocated to the #GtkAlignment.
The values can range from 0 (meaning the child doesn’t expand at all) to
1 (meaning the child expands to fill all of the available space).

The align settings are used to place the child widget within the available
area. The values range from 0 (top or left) to 1 (bottom or right).
Of course, if the scale settings are both set to 1, the alignment settings
have no effect.

GtkAlignment has been deprecated in 3.14 and should not be used in
newly-written code. The desired effect can be achieved by using the
#GtkWidget:halign, #GtkWidget:valign and #GtkWidget:margin properties on the
child widget.</doc>
